  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nLingering cloud cover has held temperatures down several degrees so \nfar today with 2 pm readings ranging from the low 70s to near 80 \ndegrees. Clouds will slowly erode from west to east through early \nevening, though temperatures have been further reduced with highs \nthrough the remainder of today peaking in the low 80s in western IL \nand middle to upper 70s in eastern IL. After a brief break from the \nlow clouds this evening, strong warm air advection ahead of an \napproaching warm front will bring another period of low stratus \novernight into Sunday morning as moisture gets trapped beneath a low-\nlevel inversion. Pockets of lower visibility are also possible with \na low to medium chance (20-40%) for dense fog.\n\nMid-level heights rise heading into Sunday as the upper ridge \namplifies over the west-central US, sending 20-25C 850 mb \ntemperatures into the middle Mississippi Valley. Surface \ntemperatures will surge into the upper 80s to low 90s on Sunday, and \nthen the low to middle 90s on Monday. Dewpoints in the upper 70s to \n80 degrees will equate to heat indices of 100 to 110 degrees the \nnext two days with Monday being the hottest. A Heat Advisory remains \nin effect for counties west of I-57 Sunday afternoon through Monday \nwhere confidence has remained highest in criteria being met (heat \nindex 105 degrees). An expansion eastward may be needed for some on \nMonday.\n\nOne complicating factor to the heat is convection and cloud cover. \nCentral Illinois is positioned on the eastern periphery of the upper \nridge, making us a prime target for northwest flow/shortwave trough \nactivity. Our first opportunity comes during the day on Sunday \nif the wave is strong enough to overcome our capping inversion. \nAdditional opportunities exist Sunday night through Monday night\nas a couple more shortwaves and an approaching cold front serve\nas our forcing mechanisms. However, placement and timing of \nactivity remains highly uncertain with CAMs providing little \nhelp. Any storms that do develop will have the potential to \nbecome severe as they move through an environment characterized \nby strong instability (3500+ J/kg SBCAPE) and sufficient \nvertical wind shear (~30 kts). Hazards with any severe storms \nwould be damaging winds, large hail, and heavy rainfall.\n\nTemperatures and dewpoints drop closer to seasonal normals for the \nmiddle to end of the week behind a cold front. Northwest flow \nremains positioned aloft through the upcoming week and may open the \ndoor for occasional precipitation opportunities through next \nweekend. However, details remain uncertain.",
